<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    Hardened Runtime entitlements for ostler-assistant.

    The daemon is signed with `codesign` plus the `runtime`
    option (release/sign-and-notarize.sh applies that flag) which
    enables the Hardened Runtime. Hardened Runtime is restrictive
    by default; we EXPLICITLY enumerate every restriction we
    accept rather than emitting an empty plist, so a future
    reviewer can see at a glance which capabilities the daemon
    does and does not require.

    Codebase audit (2026-05-02 against ostler-ai/ostler-assistant
    main):

      No JIT: a grep for `PROT_EXEC|jit|JIT` returns only
        "jitter" string matches in backoff helpers (qq.rs,
        nostr.rs, slack.rs); no executable-memory allocation.
        Therefore allow-jit and allow-unsigned-executable-memory
        stay FALSE (default secure).

      No dyld injection: a grep for `dlopen|libloading::Library`
        returns nothing. We link statically against bundled deps
        and dynamically against system libs only.
        Therefore disable-library-validation and
        allow-dyld-environment-variables stay FALSE (default
        secure).

      Network access: reqwest + tokio TcpListener (gateway).
        No App Sandbox entitlement needed; network access is
        only sandbox-gated, not Hardened-Runtime-gated.

      Apple Events to Messages.app: we shell out to /usr/bin/
        osascript via tokio::process::Command (see
        crates/zeroclaw-channels/src/imessage.rs). osascript is
        the entitled binary; our process is the parent. No
        com.apple.security.automation.apple-events needed; the
        operator grants Automation permission to osascript via
        System Settings on first run.

      File access: ~/Library/Messages/chat.db (iMessage),
        ~/Library/Mail (email-ingest). Both are TCC-protected
        (Full Disk Access, not entitlement-gated). Operator
        grants FDA via System Settings.

      Keychain: a grep for `SecKeychain|kSecClass` returns
        nothing in the workspace. Secrets are persisted via
        the in-tree ChaCha20-Poly1305 (`enc2:`) scheme in
        crates/zeroclaw-config/src/secrets.rs, not via the
        macOS Keychain. No entitlement needed.

    If a future change adds JIT (provider runtime), dlopen
    (plugin loader), or in-process AppleEvents, flip the
    relevant key here and re-sign. The release/SIGNING.md
    runbook lists the trip-wires.

    British English throughout.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <false/>

    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <false/>

    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <false/>

    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
    <false/>

    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-executable-page-protection</key>
    <false/>
</dict>
</plist>
